<Base title="Über" description="About Me & De.">
  <article>
    <header class="post-head">
      <h1>Über</h1>
      <p class="post-meta">
        <span class="sq" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>about, roughly</span>
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I am learning German. You get to read all the gems I find along the way.
        To an english speaker, German can be a very funny yet also extremely
        insightful language.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is why we have both a "Word of the day" and a "Funny word of the day".
      </p>
      <p>
        Posts are short and sweet.
      </p>
      <p>
        It might excite you to know that Mark Twain was fascinated with
        the German language and wrote a number of very good diatribes about it.
        The canon, courtesy of Project Gutenberg:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/119/pg119-images.html#Appendix_D">The Awful German Language</a>
          — Appendix D of <em>A Tramp Abroad</em>, 1880
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3188/pg3188-images.html#link2H_4_0008">Die Schrecken der deutschen Sprache</a>
          — address to the Vienna Press Club, 1897; delivered in German, rendered here in literal translation
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3188/pg3188-images.html#link2H_4_0009">German for the Hungarians</a>
          — address in Budapest, 1899
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3188/pg3188-images.html#link2H_4_0010">A New German Word</a>
          — Vienna, 1899
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
